% ============================================================================
% light-behaviors — the ways a light wave interacts with matter and what the
% source says each one DOES to the light (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of optics, and a rung a PHYSICS agent reasons UP
% from: when a light wave meets an object it does not just "hit" it — it does
% one of a small handful of NAMED things, and each named behavior has a
% characteristic effect the source states in plain words. "Reflection is when
% light bounces off; refraction is when it changes direction crossing into a new
% medium; absorption is when photons make atoms vibrate; diffraction is the
% bending and spreading of waves around an obstacle; scattering is bouncing off
% in a variety of directions." Before you can reason about a mirror, a lens, a
% prism, or why the sky is blue you must first know WHICH of these behaviors is
% happening. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? light_behavior(reflection, $Effect)      % bounces_off
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `light_behavior(behavior, effect)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% effect WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these light behaviors (it never invents an effect).
%
% Each way light interacts with matter and the effect the source states for it,
% as a little truth table:
%
%     behavior      effect (source's word)      a beginner's cue
%     -----------   -------------------------   -------------------------------
%     reflection    bounces_off                 hits a surface and bounces off
%     refraction    changes_direction           bends crossing into a new medium
%     absorption    vibrate                     photons make atoms vibrate
%     diffraction   bends_and_spreads           bends and spreads round an edge
%     scattering    variety_of_directions       bounces off every which way
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an effect and recall its behavior:
%
%     ? light_behavior($Behavior, bounces_off)   % reflection
%
% A word that is NOT one of these light behaviors — `gravity`, `magnetism`,
% `polarization` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a behavior recall ABSTAINS on
% it rather than inventing an effect. That honest-abstention property is what
% makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% behavior→effect pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `effect` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% effect the source EXPLICITLY states for that behavior — never one the source
% does not support. (Where the everyday word differs from the source's word —
% the source says photons "cause them to vibrate", not "absorb the light"; it
% says light "change[s] direction", not "bends" — the atom uses the source's
% own word, so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every behavior→effect pair
% below was read out of a fetched NASA "Wave Behaviors" page, and any pair whose
% effect could not be verified there was dropped). Each pair, with the primary
% source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   reflection → bounces_off
%     https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Reflection is when incident light (incoming light) hits an object and
%      bounces off."
%
%   refraction → changes_direction
%     https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Refraction is when light waves change direction as they pass from one
%      medium to another."
%
%   absorption → vibrate
%     https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Absorption occurs when photons from incident light hit atoms and
%      molecules and cause them to vibrate."
%
%   diffraction → bends_and_spreads
%     https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Diffraction is the bending and spreading of waves around an obstacle."
%
%   scattering → variety_of_directions
%     https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Scattering occurs when light bounces off an object in a variety of
%      directions."
%
% (The set itself is named on the same page's opening sentence — "When a light
% wave encounters an object, they are either transmitted, reflected, absorbed,
% refracted, polarized, diffracted, or scattered..." — another span of the same
% primary NASA teaching resource. `transmission` is listed there but the page
% gives it NO defining sentence of what it does to the light, so it is
% deliberately DROPPED — never grounded from memory.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NASA statement that fixes the
% first row (reflection → bounces_off). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and
% verbatim span is listed above, so each effect remains independently checkable.
% All sources are NASA Science, a primary U.S. government (NASA) teaching
% resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table light_behavior {
    columns behavior, effect

    row (reflection, bounces_off)
    row (refraction, changes_direction)
    row (absorption, vibrate)
    row (diffraction, bends_and_spreads)
    row (scattering, variety_of_directions)

    source "Reflection is when incident light (incoming light) hits an object and bounces off."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/ems/03_behaviors/"
    trust authoritative
}
